            "license": "public domain",
            "source_url": "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Chinese_Classics/Volume_1/The_Doctrine_of_the_Mean",
            "text": "Sincerity is that whereby self-completion is effected, and its way is that by which man must direct himself.",
            "translator": "Legge",
            "year": 1885
          }
        ],
        "character_count": 10,
        "id": "chapter-025-line-001",
        "order": 1,
        "source_block_chunk": 1,
        "source_block_order": 1,
        "text": "誠者自成也，而道自道也。"
      },
      {
        "canonical_translations": [
          {
            "license": "public domain",
            "source_url": "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Chinese_Classics/Volume_1/The_Doctrine_of_the_Mean",
            "text": "Sincerity is the end and beginning of things; without sincerity there would be nothing.",
            "translator": "Legge",
            "year": 1885
          }
        ],
        "character_count": 18,
        "id": "chapter-025-line-002",
        "order": 2,
        "source_block_chunk": 1,
        "source_block_order": 2,
        "text": "誠者物之終始，不誠無物。是故君子誠之為貴。"
      },
      {
        "canonical_translations": [
          {
            "license": "public domain",
            "source_url": "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Chinese_Classics/Volume_1/The_Doctrine_of_the_Mean",
            "text": "On this account, the superior man regards the attainment of sincerity as the most excellent thing.",
            "translator": "Legge",
            "year": 1885
          }
        ],
        "character_count": 38,
        "id": "chapter-025-line-003",
        "order": 3,
        "source_block_chunk": 1,
        "source_block_order": 3,
        "text": "誠者非自成己而已也，所以成物也。成己，仁也；成物，知也。性之德也，合外內之道也，故時措之宜也。"
      }
    ],
    "summary": "",
